%[text] # comp.BassReflex Class
%[text] **J.G. Vermond, JVsound**
%[text] Bass-reflex enclosure: the rear of the diaphragm works on a closed volume with a port, and the diaphragm and the port radiate.
%[text] **Namespace:** comp
%[text] **Superclasses:** [comp.Enclosure](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','enclosure','enclosuredoc.m')))
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] **Draft.** This page is a first design of the acoustical model; the class is not implemented yet. The `comp.BassReflex` class will model a bass-reflex enclosure: the rear of the diaphragm of the driver in `Driver` works on the air in a closed volume, which is open to the surroundings through a port. At low frequency the air in the port resonates with the compliance of the box, at the tuning frequency $ f\_b $. The diaphragm and the port both radiate into a half space.
%%
%[text] ### Acoustical model
%[text] The acoustical side is the two-port network $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $ with the 4-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $ of the enclosure and the 4-port $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $ of the radiation, on the front conductor (top), the ambient pressure (middle, the reference) and the rear conductor (bottom), as for [`comp.ClosedBox`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','closedbox','closedboxdoc.m'))):
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:b1r5)
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $: on the rear conductor, the acoustic mass $ M\_{a1} = \\frac{M\_{mi}}{S\_d^2} $ of the air that moves with the rear of the diaphragm in series, the compliance $ C\_a = \\frac{V\_r}{\\rho c^2} $ of the box to the ambient pressure, and the acoustic mass of the air in the port in series, $ M\_{a2} = \\frac{\\rho \\, (l\_p + \\Delta l)}{S\_p} $, with the length $ l\_p $, the area $ S\_p = \\pi r\_p^2 $ and the end correction $ \\Delta l $ of the inner end of the port. The front conductor passes through.
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $, losses: the leakage resistance $ R\_{a1} $ of the box as a shunt to the ambient pressure, the absorption resistance $ R\_{a2} $ of the box (damping material) in series with $ C\_a $, and the resistance $ R\_{a3} $ of the port in series with $ M\_{a2} $. How the losses are given, for example with the quality factors $ Q\_L $, $ Q\_A $ and $ Q\_P $ of Small, is still to be decided.
%[text] - $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $: the radiation impedances of the diaphragm, $ Z\_{rad1} $, on the front conductor, and of the outer end of the port, $ Z\_{rad2} $, on the rear conductor, both to the ambient pressure. As a first model, both are rigid pistons in an infinite baffle: `zarad` with the radius $ r\_d $ of the diaphragm and $ r\_p $ of the port. \
%[text] The volume velocity that $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $ leads to the ambient pressure is the radiated volume velocity $ U\_{rad} $ of `lspsys.solve2PortNetwork`: the sum of the flows of the diaphragm and of the port, each with its own phase. Without the radiation masses and the losses, the tuning frequency is
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ f\_b = \\frac{1}{2 \\pi \\sqrt{M\_{a2} C\_a}} $
%[text] **Open points of the design:** the end correction $ \\Delta l $ (flanged or unflanged inner end), how the losses $ R\_{a1} $, $ R\_{a2} $ and $ R\_{a3} $ are given (quality factors or resistances, and their defaults), and whether the port radiation is close enough to the diaphragm to use one position for the pressure at the microphone.
%%
%[text] ## Creation
%[text] Not implemented yet. The planned syntax is `obj = comp.BassReflex`, which creates a bass-reflex enclosure with default dimensions and a default driver.
%%
%[text] ## Properties
%[text] Planned properties (not implemented yet):
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[150,150,-1]}
%[text] | Property | Symbol | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `RearVolume` | $ V\_r $ | Volume of the box in m3 |
%[text] | `PortRadius` | $ r\_p $ | Radius of the port in m |
%[text] | `PortLength` | $ l\_p $ | Length of the port in m |
%[text] | `Driver` | | Driver that is mounted in the enclosure, inherited from `comp.Enclosure` |
%[text:table]
%%
%[text] ## Methods
%[text] Planned: `tae` and `tarad` with the elements above; `zaFront`, `zaRear` and `micTransfer` are inherited from `comp.Enclosure`, like `zarad` and `struve`. See [`solve2PortNetwork`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','lspsys','methods','solve2portnetworkdoc','solve2portnetworkdoc.m'))) for how the 4-ports are reduced.

